    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1945, state officials authorized a 36‑acre park that would become Point State Park, with the first parcel bought the next year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dcnr/recreation/where-to-go/state-parks/find-a-park/point-state-park/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Point State Park was dedicated in 1974, preserving the historic Forks of the Ohio where George Washington once passed through.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dcnr/recreation/where-to-go/state-parks/find-a-park/point-state-park/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fort Pitt, built by the British in 1759, became the most extensive fortification in North America at the time.",
